    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56081,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56082,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most gastrointestinal perineuriomas occur in the large bowel, with only rare occurrences in the small bowel or stomach.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56083,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most intestinal perineuriomas are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally during colorectal cancer screening. The rectosigmoid colon is the most common site. Occasionally, the tumours can present with recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding (gastric location).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56084,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastrointestinal perineuriomas occur in middle-aged adults, with a female predominance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56085,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56086,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "<p>Similar to schwannomas, most perineuriomas harbour chromosome&nbsp;22 deletions or monosomies, probably targeting the <em>NF2</em> tumour suppressor gene. The molecular pathogenetic basis for colorectal mucosal perineuriomas is unknown, although associated serrated polyps harbour typical <em>BRAF</em>&nbsp;p.V600E mutations {20962618; 21836484}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-05-02T10:27:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56087,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most gastrointestinal perineuriomas present as small (0.2&ndash;0.6&nbsp;cm) colonic polyps. Less commonly, they can present as non-polypoid lesions within the submucosa.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56088,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 210,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intramucosal polypoid perineuriomas expand the lamina propria and uniformly entrap crypts. Colorectal mucosal perineuriomas are often associated with serrated epithelial polyps, including both hyperplastic polyps and sessile serrated lesions {15958849; 21836484; 23588369}. Submucosal perineuriomas appear circumscribed but can focally infiltrate into the muscularis propria and subserosa {23588369}. Similar to perineurioma of soft tissues, the gastrointestinal tumours are composed of uniform spindle cells with ovoid to elongated and tapering nuclei, as well as bipolar cytoplasmic processes. The cells are generally arranged in a diffuse solid growth, often showing a distinctive whorled architecture around crypts. Mitoses are rare or absent, and no necrosis is noted. The cells are positive for EMA (highlighting delicate cell processes), claudin-1, and/or GLUT1 {15958849}. A subset of cases also express CD34. Gastrointestinal perineuriomas are negative for S100, GFAP, KIT, SMA, and keratins.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:35:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56089,
